      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The 50/30/20 rule is the most widely used personal budgeting framework in the world. It was popularized by Harvard professor and U.S. Senator Elizabeth Warren in her book \"All Your Worth.\" It is simple, flexible, and works at any income level. The Core Formula Divide your net (after-tax) income into three categories: 50% — NEEDS (Essential expenses) 30% — WANTS (Discretionary spending) 20% — SAVINGS / DEBT REPAYMENT Category % What It Covers Goal Needs 50% Rent, food, transport, utilities, healthcare, basic clothing Survival essentials Wants 30% Restaurants, travel, entertainment, premium subscriptions, hobbies Quality of life Savings 20% Emergency fund, investments, retirement, debt repayment Financial future Adapting the Rule to Your Situation The 50/30/20 rule is a starting point, not a law. In high cost-of-living cities, rent alone may consume 40-50% of income. In that case, compress the \"Wants\" category first — never the \"Savings\" category. The Non-Negotiable Rule: • The 20% savings target is the floor, not the ceiling. • If Needs exceed 50%, reduce Wants — not Savings. • If you have high-interest debt, redirect part of Wants to debt repayment. Core principle: Pay yourself first. Move your 20% to savings the moment your paycheck arrives. Then live on what remains."
