      "text": "The emergency fund is the single most important foundation in personal finance. It is not an investment. It is not savings for a vacation. It is your financial safety net — the buffer that absorbs life's unexpected punches without sending you into debt. Definition An emergency fund is money set aside in a separate, easily accessible account reserved strictly for genuine financial emergencies. The target size is 3 to 6 months of your total living expenses. What Counts as a Real Emergency? ¤ Job loss Covers living expenses while you find new employment ¤ Medical emergency Unexpected illness, surgery, or accident costs ¤ Essential repairs Car breakdown, burst pipe, broken heating system ¤ Family crisis Urgent travel, supporting a family member in need ¤ Home emergency Appliance failure, roof leak, electrical problem How Much Should It Be? Your Situation Recommended Size Reason Single, renting 3 months of expenses More flexibility to act quickly Family, mortgage 6 months of expenses More financial obligations Freelancer / self-employed 6-12 months of expenses Irregular income stream Single income household 6 months of expenses Higher risk if income stops How to Build It — Step by Step Step 1: Set a starter goal Aim for 1 month of expenses first. A small win builds momentum and proves it is possible."
