      "text": "Debt is finance's double-edged sword. Used correctly, it accelerates wealth. Used carelessly, it traps people in years of financial servitude. Understanding the difference between good and bad debt is one of the most valuable things you can learn. Good Debt vs Bad Debt Good Debt Debt taken on something that creates future value or generates income. • Education loan (increases earning power) • Business loan (when ROI > interest rate) • Mortgage (on reasonable terms) Rule: Expected return > cost of debt Bad Debt Debt taken on things that lose value immediately. • Consumer electronics on credit • Clothing, furniture on installment • Credit card balance (high interest) Rule: The item depreciates faster than you pay interest Calculating the Real Cost of a Loan Most people only look at the monthly payment when taking a loan. The real cost — total interest paid — is often shockingly large and almost always ignored. Total Interest Cost Formula Total Interest = (Monthly Payment x Number of Months) − Loan Principal This single number tells you the true price of borrowing. Loan Amount Annual Rate Term Monthly Payment Total Interest Cost $5,000 12% 12 months ~$444 ~$328 $5,000 24% 12 months ~$475 ~$700 $5,000 24% 36 months ~$196 ~$2,056 $10,000 18% 48 months ~$294 ~$4,112 Notice: a $10,000 loan at 18% over 4 years costs over $4,000 in interest alone — that is 41% of the original loan, paid just for the privilege of borrowing. How to Escape the Debt Spiral → The Avalanche Method (Most efficient) Pay minimums on all debts. Put every extra dollar toward the HIGHEST interest rate debt first. Mathematically optimal — saves the most money in total interest paid."
